hesis of B1.1 but does not conclusively favor B1.2, as systemic explanations remain speculative."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of pancreatic insufficiency (normal bowel movements, appropriate weight gain) strongly contradict the cystic fibrosis hypothesis (B1.2), given that typical CF presentations include m…", "summary": "Normal bowel function and appropriate weight gain strongly argue against classical cystic fibrosis (B1.2), but atypical variants remain minimally possible."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of specific physical exam findings for tracheobronchomegaly (e.g., hyperresonant chest on percussion, inspiratory wheeze) argue against B1.3 as the definitive diagnosis, particularly…", "summary": "Lack of characteristic exam findings for tracheobronchomegaly (B1.3) reduces its likelihood, favoring systemic conditions like B1.2 over structural explanations."}, {"t": 4, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the failure to thrive since birth and chronic airway dilation on MRI support the atypical cystic fibrosis hypothesis (B1.2), as these features are consistent with systemic involvement even witho…", "summary": "The presence of failure to thrive and chronic airway dilation provides weak support for an atypical cystic fibrosis hypothesis (B1.2), though normal bowel function and weight gain remain strong arguments against classical CF. This finding moderately reinforces the broader systemic condition hypothe…"}, {"t": 5, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the constellation of chronic airway dilation and systemic features like failure to thrive since birth support a developmental/constitutional condition (B2) as the underlying mechanism, even with…", "summary": "Failure to thrive and chronic airway dilation provide minor support for a constitutional condition (B2) as a systemic explanation, though normal bowel function limits this effect."}, {"t": 5, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETT